Biomimetic Gigascale Construction Robots

A Rigorous Framework for Morphology Optimisation, Flexible‑Body Control, and Validation

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek 

Version: 3.0 


Abstract

This thesis develops a first‑principles, computationally tractable methodology for the morphological and control co‑design of large‑scale autonomous construction robots. Rather than assuming humanoid or conventional forms, the morphology is derived as the solution to a constrained optimisation problem governed by scaling laws, structural mechanics, dynamic stability, and energy budgets. The work focuses on a hexapodal platform with two 7‑DOF anthropomorphic arms, tasked with assembling large steel structures on unprepared terrain. The objective is formalised as a bi‑objective Pareto problem maximising manipulation utility U subject to an energy cost‑of‑transport budget, with additional constraints enforcing precision, ground pressure, and structural integrity. The framework explicitly addresses non‑convexity, dynamic stability during gait transitions, structural flexibility via Craig‑Bampton modal reduction, and robustness to environmental uncertainty. A central result is a proven morphology phase transition: beyond a critical mass, bipedal configurations become infeasible, and hexapods dominate due to fault tolerance and ground pressure distribution. Validation is planned through high‑fidelity simulation and a sub‑scale 150 kg physical demonstrator, with success criteria derived from uncertainty propagation and international welding tolerance standards. The outcome is the foundational theoretical and computational framework for Gigascale Autonomous Construction Systems (GACS).

---

1. Introduction and Motivation

Modern construction faces a productivity and safety crisis. Robotics promises a solution, yet current designs either miniaturise existing heavy machinery or imitate the human form. Both strategies ignore the square‑cube law: mass grows as L^3 while structural strength scales only as L^2. For a system that must lift, place, and weld multi‑tonne components while traversing uneven ground, a humanoid morphology quickly becomes infeasible. Nature solved analogous problems over evolutionary time — birds evolved hollow, thin‑walled bones; sauropods developed columnar, allometric limbs; and arthropods exploited multi‑legged stability. These are not mere analogies; they are instances of the same underlying physical optimisation that a rational design process can now compute.

This thesis proposes to compute the optimal morphology for a gigascale construction robot from first principles. We define three scale regimes to bound the discussion:

Regime Mass Dominant Challenge Biological Analogue
Mesoscale 0.1 – 1 t Precision and agility Human
Macroscale 1 – 100 t Energy and stability Elephant, sauropod
Gigascale  100 t Ground pressure, structural scaling None (engineering only)

The framework is developed for the macroscale/gigascale transition, and the demonstrator operates at the upper mesoscale. Scaling laws, rigorously derived, justify extrapolation of conclusions to hundreds of tonnes.

Narrowed scope. To make the PhD tractable, we study one concrete mission: outdoor assembly of wind turbine tower sections (steel cylinders ~50 t, bolt‑flange joining) on prepared gravel pads with moderate slopes. The candidate robot family is a hexapodal carrier with two 7‑DOF manipulator arms.

