2. Literature Review and Research Gap

We build on four bodies of work, addressing critical omissions identified in earlier drafts.

Legged locomotion scaling. Raibert’s dynamic legged robots and the ANYmal lineage (Hutter et al.) have demonstrated Cost of Transport (CoT) ≈ 0.5–0.8 for quadrupeds up to ~100 kg. No study has extended validated CoT models beyond 1 t, where structural deflection and actuator saturation dominate. Our contribution is the derivation of constant‑CoT scaling under elastic similarity, and its numerical validation in simulation.

Computational morphology co‑design. Ha et al. (2018) introduced a framework for optimising robot morphology from high‑level task specifications using differentiable simulation. Wensing, Wang, and Dreifuss (2023) advanced co‑design for dynamic legged robots. These works treat limbs as rigid bodies; they do not incorporate lattice material parameterisation, hollow‑member topology, or construction‑specific payload/precision constraints. We extend co‑design to the gigascale by integrating Gibson‑Ashby material models, SIMP topology optimisation, and structural flexibility in the loop.

Biomimetic structural mechanics. Avian bone and sauropod allometry are well quantified. McMahon’s elastic similarity and Gibson‑Ashby cellular solid laws are mature, but they have never been embedded in a full‑body morphology optimiser that simultaneously respects dynamic stability and task utility. Our work closes this gap.

Construction and space robotics. The Hadrian X bricklayer, ETH’s In situ Fabricator, and NASA’s RASSOR are point solutions. None addresses the morphological optimisation of a multi‑tonne, multi‑limb autonomous builder. Our framework is the first to treat the robot’s body as a design variable co‑equal with its controller.

Terramechanics. Constraint (C8) on ground pressure is derived from Bekker‑Wong pressure‑sinkage models for granular soils, explicitly parameterised by cohesion and friction angle. This ensures the framework is physically valid for lunar/Martian regolith, not just terrestrial gravel.

