4. Mathematical Foundations

All symbols retain their standard meanings unless redefined.

4.1 Square‑Cube Law and Allometric Scaling

We restate Theorems 4.1–Corollary 4.1 from the original draft but add the essential allometric response. To avoid the efficiency collapse \eta \propto 1/L, the limb cross‑section must grow faster than geometric similarity allows. McMahon’s elastic similarity criterion for buckling‑resistant columns requires:

d \propto l^{3/2} \quad \Longrightarrow \quad I \propto L^5, \quad A \propto L^3.

This makes \eta constant, a design rule enforced as constraint (C7) in our optimisation.

4.2 Hollow Member Mechanics (Avian Principle)

The specific bending stiffness advantage of a thin‑walled tube over a solid bar of equal mass is R/t (Theorem 4.2). For a gigascale arm link of radius 0.3 m and wall thickness 5 mm, this advantage is 60×. The detailed bending stress and torsional shear formulas are unchanged. The design sub‑problem selects (R, t) per link to minimise mass subject to a bending failure safety factor SF = 2.0 and a local buckling limit for the thin wall.

4.3 Lattice Material Selection via Gibson‑Ashby

The effective modulus and strength of a lattice of relative density \bar{\rho} are given by the power‑law relations of Section 4.4 (original). For each structural member, the optimal \bar{\rho} solves the mass‑minimisation problem under stiffness and strength constraints, as previously stated. This is evaluated analytically for candidate lattice topologies (octet‑truss, Kelvin foam) pre‑selected for additive manufacturability.

4.4 Topology Optimisation (SIMP) — Local Refinement

The SIMP formulation (Problem 4.1) is applied to each link envelope independently, using the worst‑case dynamic wrench from the full‑body trajectory optimiser as the load. This produces a manufacturable density distribution that respects the hollow‑wall constraint and serves as the biomimetic “bone” interior.

4.5 Stability: ZMP with Gait Transitions

The support polygon S(t) varies with the gait phase. We define the gait‑cycle minimum stability margin:

\Omega_{\text{gait}} = \min_{t \in [0, T_{\text{cycle}}]} d\big(p_{\text{ZMP}}(t), \partial S(t)\big).

The dynamic stability constraint (C2) becomes \Omega_{\text{gait}} \ge \Omega_{\text{crit}} > 0, with \Omega_{\text{crit}} set to 5 cm to provide a safety margin against sensor noise and ground unevenness. This correctly captures stability during leg transfers.

4.6 Floating‑Base Dynamics and Centroidal Momentum

The full‑body equations are unchanged. The centroidal dynamics:

\dot{p}_c = mg \hat{z} + \sum_i f_i, \qquad \dot{L}_c = \sum_i (r_i - r_c) \times f_i,

are used in the whole‑body balance controller (Level 2). The contact forces f_i must satisfy the Coulomb friction cone and a pressure limit derived from terramechanics:

p_i = \frac{f_{i,z}}{A_{\text{foot}}} \le p_{\text{ult}} = c N_c + \gamma z N_q + 0.5 \gamma B N_\gamma,

where c, \gamma are soil cohesion and unit weight, B the footing width, and N_c, N_q, N_\gamma are bearing capacity factors (Terzaghi). This replaces the simplistic p \le p_{\text{soil}}.

4.7 Energy Model — Rigorous Constant‑CoT Theorem

Theorem 4.5 (Scale‑Invariant CoT under Elastic and Froude Similarity).
Assume the robot’s limbs satisfy elastic similarity d \propto l^{3/2} and that gaits are operated at a constant Froude number Fr = v^2/(gL). Then the dimensionless Cost of Transport is independent of characteristic length L.

Proof.

1. Mass: m \propto \rho L^3 (from A \propto L^3).
2. Moment of inertia of a limb segment about its joint: I \propto m L^2 \propto L^5.
3. Under gravitational load, the natural pendular frequency of the limb scales as \omega \propto \sqrt{mgL / I} \propto \sqrt{L^3 \cdot L / L^5} = L^{-1/2}.
4. Froude similarity requires v \propto \sqrt{gL}, hence stride frequency f \propto v/L \propto L^{-1/2}, matching \omega. Dimensionless joint speeds \dot{q}/\omega are thus invariant with size.
5. Joint torque required to support body weight scales as \tau \propto mgL \propto L^4. Therefore dimensionless torque \bar{\tau} = \tau/(mgL) \propto L^0.
6. The work per stride is W = \int |\tau \dot{q}| dt \propto (mgL) \cdot \omega \cdot (1/f) \propto L^4 \cdot L^{-1/2} \cdot L^{1/2} = L^4.
7. The distance travelled per stride is \Delta x \propto L. The weight is mg \propto L^3. Thus

\text{CoT} = \frac{W}{mg \Delta x} \propto \frac{L^4}{L^3 \cdot L} = L^0.

Hence CoT is independent of size, provided elastic similarity and Froude similarity are maintained. ∎

This proof replaces the earlier sketch and directly supports the biomimetic limb design constraint (C7).

4.8 Manipulation Utility and Task‑Dependent Weights

The utility functional is redefined with explicit normalisation and sensitivity analysis:

U = \alpha \left(\frac{W}{W_{\text{ref}}}\right) + \beta \left(\frac{D}{D_{\text{ref}}}\right) + \gamma \left(\frac{P_{\text{ref}}}{P}\right),

where W is maximum payload mass, D the dexterous workspace volume, P = \sigma_{\text{pos}} the positioning standard deviation. The reference values and weights are derived from the chosen construction task. For wind turbine tower assembly:

· Required lift capacity: 60 t → W_{\text{ref}} = 60\,\text{t}.
· Workspace: a cylindrical envelope of radius 5 m, height 8 m → D_{\text{ref}} \approx 628\,\text{m}^3.
· Positioning tolerance: AWS D1.1 root opening for full‑penetration groove welds = ±2 mm → P_{\text{ref}} = 2\,\text{mm}.

The weights (\alpha, \beta, \gamma) are then determined by a structured sensitivity analysis across the simplex \alpha+\beta+\gamma=1, showing how the optimal morphology shifts as priorities change. This replaces the ad‑hoc tuning.

4.9 Structural Flexibility Formalisation — Craig‑Bampton Reduction

To enable precision control without excessive structural mass, we explicitly model the flexibility of lattice limbs. Each arm link is treated as a linear elastic body with finite‑element discretisation. Using the Craig‑Bampton method, the link’s dynamics are reduced to boundary DOFs (joint interfaces) and a set of k fixed‑interface normal modes \phi_j:

u_{\text{int}} = \Phi \eta,

where \eta \in \mathbb{R}^k are modal coordinates. The end‑effector deflection is:

\delta_{\text{ee}} = \sum_{j=1}^{k} \phi_j(x_{\text{ee}}) \,\eta_j.

The modal equations are:

\ddot{\eta}_j + 2\zeta_j \omega_j \dot{\eta}_j + \omega_j^2 \eta_j = \phi_j^T F_{\text{applied}}.

In the hierarchical controller (Level 3), the desired end‑effector trajectory is pre‑distorted by the predicted quasi‑static deflection:

x_{\text{ee, cmd}} = x_{\text{ee, desired}} + \delta_{\text{ee, predicted}},

where \delta_{\text{ee, predicted}} is computed from the current payload estimate and the known modal compliance. This turns constraint (C9) from a vague bound into a physically actionable compensation law. The number of retained modes k is chosen such that the total effective mass exceeds 95% of the link’s mass, ensuring accuracy without excessive computational load.

4.10 Robust and Fault‑Tolerant Formulation

Construction sites are stochastic. We introduce an uncertainty set \Delta containing:

· Wind gusts up to 30 m/s (Beaufort 8, as a stop‑work limit).
· Payload mass uncertainty ±5%.
· Terrain elevation roughness ±10 cm.

The robust optimisation problem augments the constraints to hold for all \delta \in \Delta:

g_j(\Psi, \delta) \le 0 \quad \forall \delta \in \Delta.

Additionally, we formalise a fault‑tolerance constraint (C10). For a hexapod, after the loss of any single leg (worst‑case), the robot must remain statically stable with a reduced support polygon:

\text{FT}(\Psi) = \min_{i \in \{1,\dots,6\}} \Omega_{\text{gait, 5-legs}}(\Psi \setminus \{\text{leg}_i\}) \ge \Omega_{\text{crit}}.

This constraint is impossible for a quadruped (only three legs remain), mathematically establishing the hexapod advantage as a fault‑tolerance theorem.

