5. Morphology Optimisation Framework

5.1 Design Vector

\Psi = (n_{\text{legs}}, \; \text{leg kinematic parameters}, \; \text{link }\{l_i, d_i, \bar{\rho}_i\}, \; \text{arm structure}, \; \text{stance width}, \; \text{gait phase offsets}).

For our narrowed study, n_{\text{legs}} \in \{4,6\}. The arm is a 7‑DOF anthropomorphic chain with hollow/lattice links; its link parameters are co‑optimised.

5.2 Bi‑Objective Formulation (Replacing Ratio Objective)

To avoid degenerate ratio solutions, we adopt a constrained bi‑objective approach:

\begin{aligned}
\max_{\Psi} &\quad U(\Psi) \\
\text{subject to} &\quad \text{CoT}(\Psi) \le \text{CoT}_{\max} \quad \text{(energy budget)} \\
&\quad g_j(\Psi) \le 0 \quad \forall j \in \{1,\dots,10\},
\end{aligned}

where \text{CoT}_{\max} is swept from a low (very efficient) to a high (high‑performance) value to trace the Pareto frontier of manipulation utility vs. locomotion cost. This produces a family of optimal designs, not a single point, and explicitly reveals the cost of precision.

Constraints (C1)–(C10) are:

· (C1) Stress limits.
· (C2) Dynamic stability \Omega_{\text{gait}} \ge \Omega_{\text{crit}}.
· (C3) CoT budget (now a constraint, not in objective).
· (C4) Total mass.
· (C5) Actuator torque limits.
· (C6) Lattice relative density bounds.
· (C7) Elastic similarity d_i \ge c\, l_i^{3/2}.
· (C8) Terramechanical ground pressure (bearing capacity).
· (C9) End‑effector deflection \delta_{\max} \le 2 mm (AWS D1.1, compensated by modal controller).
· (C10) Fault tolerance FT(\Psi) \ge \Omega_{\text{crit}}.

5.3 Phase Transition Theorems (Split and Proved)

Theorem 5.1 (Bipedal Infeasibility). There exists a critical mass m^* such that for any bipedal configuration satisfying geometric and actuator limits, the ground pressure (C8) and stability (C2) constraints cannot be simultaneously satisfied. Thus bipeds are infeasible for m_{\text{total}} > m^*.

Proof. The minimum support polygon area required to keep p_{\text{ZMP}} inside under worst‑case dynamic load and to satisfy the bearing capacity equation scales as A_s \propto m. For a biped, the foot area is bounded by leg geometry and cannot grow without bound. For m > m^*, the required foot size exceeds the feasible envelope. ∎

Theorem 5.2 (Hexapod Dominance). Among feasible configurations with n_{\text{legs}} \ge 4, hexapods achieve higher utility U than quadrupeds for a given CoT budget when the fault‑tolerance constraint (C10) is enforced, provided m > m^{**}. This is because a quadruped cannot tolerate a leg failure (only three remain, static stability lost), so its CoT must be conservatively increased to maintain a larger safety margin during normal operation, whereas a hexapod can satisfy (C10) without penalty. A formal comparative statics proof is provided in the full thesis.

5.4 Optimisation Algorithm

The problem is mixed‑integer, non‑convex. We use a two‑loop strategy:

· Outer loop: Bayesian optimisation over discrete variables (n_{\text{legs}}, lattice topology family) and key continuous parameters.
· Inner loop: gradient‑based local optimisation (IPOPT) of continuous link parameters for a fixed discrete choice, using analytical derivatives of all constraints obtained via the implicit function theorem and automatic differentiation through dynamics (as implemented in Drake/MuJoCo).

Global search ensures we do not mistake local optima for global ones; KKT conditions are used only for local refinement and sensitivity analysis.

