6. Hierarchical Control Architecture with Flexibility Compensation

The four‑level HQP remains as originally formulated, but with the following critical augmentation:

Level 2 (Whole‑Body Balance): The QP tracks a desired centroidal momentum and ZMP trajectory, enforcing contact force and friction constraints. The ZMP reference is generated to maintain \Omega_{\text{gait}} \ge \Omega_{\text{crit}} over the full gait cycle.

Level 3 (Task‑Space Manipulation): The end‑effector desired acceleration is modified to include the predicted deflection compensation:

\ddot{x}_{\text{ee, cmd}} = \ddot{x}_{\text{ee, des}} + K_p (x_{\text{ee, des}} + \delta_{\text{ee, pred}} - x_{\text{ee}}) + K_d (\dot{x}_{\text{ee, des}} - \dot{x}_{\text{ee}}),

where \delta_{\text{ee, pred}} is computed online from the current payload mass estimate and the pre‑computed Craig‑Bampton modal compliance matrix. This ensures the physical end‑effector tracks the desired position despite static sag and low‑frequency oscillation.

The entire HQP is implemented in a receding‑horizon MPC fashion at 100 Hz, with the modal coordinates updated from joint‑torque‑based disturbance observers.

