7. Validation Methodology

7.1 Simulation

High‑fidelity co‑simulation: MuJoCo for rigid‑body dynamics and contact, coupled with a custom FEM solver (CalculiX or in‑house) for lattice limb flexibility via the Craig‑Bampton modes. A suite of benchmark tasks (lift‑and‑place a 100 kg scaled‑down steel cylinder, weld a seam) will be performed across the Pareto‑optimal morphologies. The simulation validates the scaling law (Theorem 4.5), the phase transition, and the deflection compensation accuracy.

7.2 Physical Demonstrator

A mesoscale prototype: hexapod body ~2 m long, ∼150 kg, with two 5‑DOF arms (simplified for cost). One arm link will be a hollow titanium lattice structure built by laser powder bed fusion, designed by the SIMP + Gibson‑Ashby pipeline. Tests:

· Payload test: Lift a mass equal to 80% of actuator limit at full reach; measure end‑effector deflection with a laser tracker. Goal: deflection ≤ 2 mm after compensation (AWS D1.1 scaled).
· CoT measurement: Walk on flat ground at 0.5 m/s, measure electrical power. Joint torque sensors (ATI Mini45) with ±2% accuracy, encoder resolution 0.01°. Propagate uncertainty through the CoT integral to obtain a 3σ confidence interval. The model prediction must fall within this interval.
· Fault tolerance: With one leg locked, verify static stability margin Ω > 0.05 m.

Success criteria (quantitative):

· CoT experimental mean ± 3σ contains the model’s predicted value.
· End‑effector RMS deflection under maximum payload ≤ 2.0 mm.
· Post‑failure static stability margin ≥ 0.05 m.
  These criteria are directly linked to physical standards and measurement capabilities, not arbitrary percentages.

7.3 Extrapolation to Gigascale

We argue that the scaling law (Theorem 4.5) and the similarity conditions are valid from meso‑ to gigascale as long as the elastic similarity and Froude similarity are maintained, and as long as the material remains in the linear‑elastic regime (which it does for the chosen titanium alloy). This will be supported by a dimensionless sensitivity analysis in simulation at 1 t and 50 t scale models, showing collapse of CoT and structural efficiency curves.

