Appendix D: Stability and Dynamics

D.1 Support Polygon and Static Stability

Definition D.1 (Support Polygon).
For ground contact points \{p_1,\dots,p_n\} \subset \mathbb{R}^2,

S = \operatorname{conv}\{p_1,\dots,p_n\} = \Bigl\{ \sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i p_i : \lambda_i \ge 0,\; \sum_i \lambda_i = 1 \Bigr\}.

Theorem D.1 (Static Stability).
A legged robot in static equilibrium is stable iff the ground projection of its centre of mass p_{\text{CoM}} lies strictly inside S.

Proof.
Force and moment equilibrium require \sum f_i = mg\,\hat{z} and \sum (p_i - p_{\text{CoM}}) \times f_i = 0. Since f_i are unilateral (f_i\!\cdot\!\hat{z} \ge 0), the moment equation implies

p_{\text{CoM}} = \sum_i \frac{f_i\!\cdot\!\hat{z}}{mg} p_i,

so p_{\text{CoM}} is a convex combination of the p_i. Stability margin requires the strict inclusion p_{\text{CoM}} \in \operatorname{int}(S). ∎

D.2 Zero Moment Point and Dynamic Stability

Definition D.2 (ZMP).
For a robot with CoM position \mathbf{r}_c = (x_c, y_c, z_c) and acceleration \ddot{\mathbf{r}}_c,

x_{\text{ZMP}} = x_c - \frac{z_c}{g + \ddot{z}_c} \ddot{x}_c, \qquad
y_{\text{ZMP}} = y_c - \frac{z_c}{g + \ddot{z}_c} \ddot{y}_c.

Theorem D.2 (Dynamic Stability).
A dynamically moving robot is stable iff p_{\text{ZMP}} \in S at all times.

Proof.
Apply D’Alembert’s principle: inertial forces -m\ddot{\mathbf{r}}_c are added to the gravity. The ZMP is the point on the ground where the horizontal moment of all (real + inertial) forces vanishes. The same convex combination argument as in Theorem D.1 then yields that the ZMP must lie in S for the contact forces to be realisable. ∎

D.3 Gait‑Cycle Minimum Stability Margin

For a periodic gait with cycle time T_{\text{cycle}}, the support polygon S(t) varies as legs lift and place. We define the gait‑cycle minimum stability margin:

\Omega_{\text{gait}} = \min_{t \in [0, T_{\text{cycle}}]} d\!\bigl(p_{\text{ZMP}}(t), \partial S(t)\bigr).

The dynamic stability constraint becomes

\Omega_{\text{gait}} \ge \Omega_{\text{crit}} > 0,

with \Omega_{\text{crit}} chosen to exceed sensor noise and terrain irregularity.

D.4 Floating‑Base Dynamics

Let the generalised coordinates be q = (x_B, R_B, \theta) \in \mathbb{R}^3 \times SO(3) \times \mathbb{R}^n. The Euler–Lagrange equations in matrix form are

M(q)\ddot{q} + h(q,\dot{q}) = S^\mathsf{T}\tau + J_c^\mathsf{T}\lambda,

where S = [0_{n \times 6} \; I_n] selects the actuated joints, and \lambda collects the contact wrenches constrained by Coulomb friction. The centroidal momentum dynamics are

\dot{p}_c = mg\,\hat{z} + \sum_i f_i, \qquad
\dot{L}_c = \sum_i (r_i - r_c) \times f_i.

