Appendix E: Energy and Locomotion Scaling

E.1 Rigorous Constant Cost of Transport

Definition E.1 (Cost of Transport).
The dimensionless Cost of Transport for a locomotion cycle that moves the robot a horizontal distance \Delta x is

\text{CoT} = \frac{\sum_i \int |\tau_i \dot{q}_i| \, dt}{m g \Delta x}.

Theorem E.1 (Scale‑Invariant CoT under Elastic and Froude Similarity).
Assume the robot’s limbs satisfy McMahon’s elastic similarity, d \propto l^{3/2}, and that gaits are operated at a constant Froude number Fr = v^2 / (gL). Then the Cost of Transport is independent of the characteristic length L.

Proof.
(1) Mass: with A \propto L^3, m \propto \rho L^3.
(2) Limb segment moment of inertia about its joint: I \propto m L^2 \propto L^5.
(3) Natural pendular frequency: \omega \propto \sqrt{mgL / I} \propto \sqrt{L^3 \cdot L / L^5} = L^{-1/2}.
(4) Froude similarity imposes v \propto \sqrt{gL}, so stride frequency f \propto v/L \propto L^{-1/2}. Hence the dimensionless joint speed \dot{q}/\omega remains constant.
(5) Joint torque required to support body weight: \tau \propto mgL \propto L^4. Dimensionless torque \bar{\tau} = \tau/(mgL) is thus invariant.
(6) Work per stride: W \propto \int |\tau \dot{q}| dt \propto \tau \cdot (\omega) \cdot (1/f) \propto L^4 \cdot L^{-1/2} \cdot L^{1/2} = L^4.
(7) Distance per stride \Delta x \propto L, weight mg \propto L^3. Therefore

\text{CoT} = \frac{W}{mg \Delta x} \propto \frac{L^4}{L^3 \cdot L} = L^0.

Hence CoT is independent of size, provided the stated similarities hold. ∎

