Appendix F: Morphology Optimisation

F.1 Design Vector and Bi‑Objective Formulation

The morphology is encoded by

\Psi = (n_{\text{legs}}, \{l_i, d_i, \bar{\rho}_i\}, \text{arm parameters}, \text{stance width}, \text{gait phase}).

To avoid degenerate ratios, we maximise manipulation utility U (defined in Appendix G) subject to a CoT budget:

\begin{aligned}
\max_{\Psi} &\quad U(\Psi) \\
\text{s.t.} &\quad \text{CoT}(\Psi) \le \text{CoT}_{\max}, \\
&\quad g_j(\Psi) \le 0 \quad (j=1,\dots,10).
\end{aligned}

Varying \text{CoT}_{\max} traces the Pareto frontier.

F.2 KKT Conditions for Continuous Relaxation

For the continuous relaxation (fixed n_{\text{legs}}, lattice topology), the Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L}(\Psi, \mu) = -U(\Psi) + \sum_j \mu_j g_j(\Psi).

At a local optimum \Psi^*,

\nabla_\Psi U(\Psi^*) = \sum_j \mu_j^* \nabla_\Psi g_j(\Psi^*), \quad
\mu_j^* \ge 0, \quad \mu_j^* g_j(\Psi^*) = 0.

Because the problem is non‑convex, KKT conditions are used for local characterisation; global search employs Bayesian optimisation over the discrete–continuous product space.

F.3 Morphology Phase Transition Theorems

Theorem F.1 (Bipedal Infeasibility).
There exists a critical mass m^* such that any bipedal configuration satisfying geometric and actuator limits violates the ground‑pressure constraint (C8) or the dynamic stability constraint (C2) for all feasible foot geometries.

Proof.
The required support polygon area to keep p_{\text{ZMP}} inside and to satisfy the terramechanical bearing capacity p \le p_{\text{ult}} scales as A_s^{\text{req}} \propto m. For a biped, the maximum feasible foot area is bounded by leg geometry and interference constraints. Beyond the mass where A_s^{\text{req}} exceeds this bound, no bipedal solution exists. ∎

Theorem F.2 (Hexapod Dominance via Fault Tolerance).
Impose the fault‑tolerance constraint (C10): after the removal of any single leg, the reduced‑leg configuration must maintain a static stability margin \Omega \ge \Omega_{\text{crit}}. For m > m^{**}, a quadruped cannot satisfy (C10) because three legs cannot form a support polygon containing the CoM while maintaining the required ground pressure, whereas a hexapod retains at least five legs, which can. Consequently, among feasible configurations, hexapods achieve higher utility U for a given CoT budget.

Proof.
A quadruped with one leg lost has only three contact points; the support polygon degenerates to a triangle whose area is insufficient to satisfy both the ZMP condition and the bearing pressure limit at large mass. A hexapod with five legs can form a robust quadrilateral support polygon. Formal comparative statics using the constraints (C8) and (C10) yields the required mass threshold m^{**}. ∎

