Appendix G: Structural Flexibility and Precision Control

G.1 Craig–Bampton Modal Reduction

Each lattice arm link is treated as a linear elastic body. The Craig–Bampton method partitions the link’s degrees of freedom into boundary (joint interface) DOFs \mathbf{u}_B and interior DOFs \mathbf{u}_I. The interior dynamics are approximated by a truncated set of k fixed‑interface normal modes \mathbf{\Phi}:

\mathbf{u}_I \approx \mathbf{\Phi} \boldsymbol{\eta},

where \boldsymbol{\eta} \in \mathbb{R}^k are modal coordinates. The equations of motion in modal space are

\ddot{\eta}_j + 2\zeta_j \omega_j \dot{\eta}_j + \omega_j^2 \eta_j = \boldsymbol{\phi}_j^\mathsf{T} \mathbf{F}_{\text{applied}}.

The end‑effector deflection is

\boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{ee}} = \sum_{j=1}^k \boldsymbol{\phi}_j(\mathbf{x}_{\text{ee}})\,\eta_j.

G.2 Feed‑Forward Compensation in Hierarchical Control

In the Level 3 task‑space QP, the desired end‑effector acceleration is modified to include predicted deflection compensation:

\ddot{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{ee, cmd}} = \ddot{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{ee, des}} + K_p\bigl(\mathbf{x}_{\text{ee, des}} + \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{ee, pred}} - \mathbf{x}_{\text{ee}}\bigr) + K_d\bigl(\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{ee, des}} - \dot{\mathbf{x}}_{\text{ee}}\bigr),

where \boldsymbol{\delta}_{\text{ee, pred}} is computed online from the current payload mass estimate and the pre‑computed modal compliance matrix. This transforms constraint (C9) from a passive bound into an active control law, enabling the robot to meet the AWS D1.1 welding tolerance of ±2 mm.

